    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Wanyan Aguda</a> in 1115 and named Jin, 'gold'.</li><li>It destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in 1127 and ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, moving its capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1153.</li><li>Mongol invasions from 1211 drove the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, and the dynasty ended at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-caizhou">Caizhou</a> in 1234.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1127 Jin armies carried off two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> emperors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Huizong</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-qinzong">Qinzong</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, where both died in captivity.</p></aside>
    </section>

