    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin.jpg" alt="Emperor Taizu of Jin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Emperor Taizu of Jin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wanggiyan_Aguda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Jin dynasty was founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>, a Tungusic people of the forests and river valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> who lived by farming, hunting, fishing and stock-raising and paid tribute to the Khitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>. In the eleventh century the Wanyan clan, based on the Anchuhu river near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a>, united many Jurchen tribes. Resentment of Liao demands, including tribute in hunting falcons and the abuse of Jurchen communities by Liao envoys, led the Wanyan chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Aguda</a> to rebel in 1114. After victories over Liao forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ningjiang">Ningjiang</a> and Chuhedian, he proclaimed himself emperor of the Great Jin early in 1115, with his capital at Huining, later called Shangjing. The dynastic name means 'gold', traditionally linked to the Anchuhu, whose Jurchen name also meant gold. Aguda (Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Taizu</a>, r. 1115–1123) went on to rout a large army led by the Liao emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-tianzuo-of-liao">Tianzuo</a> and took the Liao Eastern Capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Liaoyang</a> in 1116. In 1120 Jin concluded the 'Alliance Conducted at Sea' with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> against Liao, and Jin armies took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangjing-of-liao">Liao Supreme Capital</a> that year and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Yanjing</a> (Beijing) in 1122, after Song forces had failed to capture it. Aguda's brother and successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-jin">Wuqimai</a> (Emperor Taizong) captured Tianzuo in 1125, extinguishing the Liao state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yellow-river.jpg" alt="Yellow River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_River_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">fading, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jin rule brought together peoples of very different ways of life. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> were a minority in an empire whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1207 counted more than 45 million people, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> farmers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>, alongside Khitan, Bohai and other peoples. To hold the conquered lands, much of the Jurchen population was resettled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a> in military-agricultural communities and given land, some of it taken from Chinese farmers, which caused lasting friction and left many Jurchen households impoverished. North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was a rich agricultural region, but war, land seizures and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, which broke its banks and changed course in 1194, brought hardship. The Jin issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> from the 1150s; heavy issues to pay for wars in the thirteenth century caused severe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>. Trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> through official border markets brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and other goods north, while horses and other northern products went south. Religious life was varied. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> enjoyed court patronage, and new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Daoist</a> movements arose in Jin territory, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhen-school">Quanzhen</a> (Complete Perfection) school founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-chongyang">Wang Chongyang</a> in the 1160s, which became one of the two main branches of Daoism. Medical scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-wansu">Liu Wansu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-congzheng">Zhang Congzheng</a> founded influential schools of Chinese medicine, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> flourished at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pingyang-shanxi">Pingyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-shizhong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/han-shizhong.jpg" alt="Han Shizhong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/han-shizhong">Han Shizhong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%9F%93%E4%B8%96%E5%BF%A0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">劉松年繪, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> military power rested on armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> organised in the <em>meng'an mouke</em> system, in which units of nominally 300 households (<em>mouke</em>) were grouped by ten into larger units (<em>meng'an</em>, 'thousand'), each providing soldiers for war. Jin cavalry overwhelmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and then turned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> after disputes over the spoils. In 1126–1127 Jin armies twice besieged the Song capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>; in the Jingkang Incident of 1127 they took the city and carried off the retired emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Huizong</a>, the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-qinzong">Qinzong</a> and thousands of members of the court. Jin forces pursued the new Southern Song court south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> in 1129–1130 but were checked by Song generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-shizhong">Han Shizhong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a>, and the Jin governed part of the conquered land through the puppet state of Qi from 1130 to 1137. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shaoxing">Treaty of Shaoxing</a> of 1141 fixed the border along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang">Prince Hailing</a>'s attempt to conquer the Song in 1161 failed when his fleet was beaten at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caishi">Battle of Caishi</a> on the Yangtze. Jin armies repelled a Song offensive in 1206–1208, but from 1211 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> proved irresistible, destroying Jin field armies at Yehuling and, in 1232, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sanfengshan">Sanfengshan</a>, despite a stubborn defence of walled cities with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons such as the iron 'thunder-crash' bombs used at Kaifeng.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang.jpg" alt="Wanyan Liang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang">Wanyan Liang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wanyan_Digunai_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Slb nsk, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early Jin state was governed by a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> nobles, the <em>bojilie</em>, in which the emperor was first among clan chiefs. The conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> lands forced rapid change. The Jin adopted Liao and Song institutions, examinations for Chinese officials and a system of several capitals, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xizong-of-jin">Emperor Xizong</a> (r. 1135–1150) and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang">Wanyan Liang</a>, Prince Hailing (r. 1150–1161), centralised power on Chinese lines. Hailing murdered his predecessor, executed many Wanyan princes, moved the capital from Huining to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>, the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in 1153, and in 1156 simplified the central government around a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/department-of-state-affairs">Department of State Affairs</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shizong-of-jin">Emperor Shizong</a> (r. 1161–1189), remembered by Chinese historians as a model ruler, made peace with the Song in 1165, reformed taxation and worried that the Jurchen were losing their identity: he promoted the Jurchen language, had Chinese classics translated into it and created examinations in Jurchen in 1173. A Jurchen script, based partly on Chinese characters and the Khitan scripts, had been created in 1119 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-xiyin">Wanyan Xiyin</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhangzong-of-jin">Zhangzong</a> (r. 1189–1208) Confucian government reached its height, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taihe-code">Taihe Code</a> was issued in 1201. The Jin emperors claimed to be legitimate rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the line of earlier dynasties, and in the treaty of 1141 the Southern Song accepted a subordinate status.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-western-chamber"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-western-chamber.jpg" alt="Romance of the Western Chamber" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-western-chamber">Romance of the Western Chamber</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%97%B5%E9%BD%90%E4%BC%8B%E7%BB%98%E5%88%BB%E8%A5%BF%E5%8E%A2%E8%AE%B0%E5%BD%A9%E5%9B%BE%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">闵齐伋, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Jin dynasty changed the political geography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. By destroying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and driving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> from the north, it divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for more than a century between a conquest state in the north and the Southern Song, whose rich economy and culture developed in the south. Its move of the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a> began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>'s history as a main imperial capital, a role continued by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Mongol Yuan</a>, the Ming and the Qing; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge">Lugou Bridge</a>, known in the West as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> Bridge, was built near Zhongdu between 1189 and 1192. Jin rule was also a creative period for culture in the north. Northern scholars kept Confucian learning alive, and the poet and historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-haowen">Yuan Haowen</a>, who lived through the fall of the dynasty, preserved its literature in his anthology <em>Zhongzhou ji</em>. Sung narrative in the <em>zhugongdiao</em> form flourished, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-jieyuan">Dong Jieyuan</a>'s version of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-western-chamber">Romance of the Western Chamber</a></em> became a source for later drama, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the Jin north led to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaju">zaju</a></em> drama of the Yuan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhen-school">Quanzhen</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Daoist</a> order spread widely. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> script survives on inscriptions and in a Ming glossary. The Jin also became a model for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>, a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jianzhou-jurchens">Jianzhou Jurchen</a>, named his state the Later Jin in 1616 before his successor renamed it Qing.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yehuling"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-yehuling.jpg" alt="Battle of Yehuling" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yehuling">Battle of Yehuling</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_entre_mongols_%26_chinois_(1211).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sayf al-Vâhidî. Hérât. Afghanistan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Jin began to weaken in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhangzong-of-jin">Zhangzong</a>, when floods, the change of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>'s course, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and the cost of war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> and on the steppe strained the state. In the north, the Mongol tribes that the Jin had long played off against one another were united by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who invaded in 1211 and destroyed a large Jin army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yehuling">Battle of Yehuling</a> (Badger Mouth). Mongol armies ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a>, a coup killed the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-yongji">Weishao Wang</a> in 1213, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuanzong-of-jin">Xuanzong</a> bought a truce in 1214 with tribute and a princess, then moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> treated the move as a breach of the truce and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a> in 1215. Reduced to the lands around the Yellow River, the Jin fought costly wars against the Song and Western Xia while the Mongol general Muqali advanced. After a respite following Genghis Khan's death in 1227, Ögedei renewed the assault: the Mongols under Tolui crushed the last Jin field army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sanfengshan">Sanfengshan</a> in 1232 and besieged Kaifeng, which surrendered in 1233. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-aizong-of-jin">Aizong</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-caizhou">Caizhou</a>, where a Mongol and Southern Song siege ended in February 1234. He abdicated and took his own life, and his successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-chenglin">Wanyan Chenglin</a>, was killed the same day, ending the dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Jin dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Khitan Liao</a>, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and ruled it for over a century, combining Jurchen military organisation with Chinese government, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> a main imperial capital and providing the model to which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> of the Qing later looked back.</em></p>

